From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Mon, 22 Jun 2026 16:07:22 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 03488/11362] [outer-loop] Hindsight goal-induction: pair
 reveals the PREDICATE but not the DENSE gradient

Hindsight breaks half the chicken-and-egg: Phase 1 blind-solved lp85 to L1, captured the
(start, win) PAIR, and the pair CLEANLY revealed the win mechanic (top bar = slot counter;
the win fills a slot) -- far more tractable than static-structure induction (sb26). BUT
Phase 2 (deepen to L2 with the pair-induced goal vs blind) NULLED: both stuck at L1
(exp=4000). The pair gives a TERMINAL goal ("fill the slots") but NOT a dense gradient for
the per-level matching sub-puzzle (which middle block fills which slot); a sparse
slot-count heuristic is flat between fills, so it guides no better than blind.

REFINED FINDING: hindsight solves "WHAT is the win" but the remaining bottleneck is the
DENSE per-level navigation -- when each level is itself a search/matching puzzle, a
terminal goal predicate is not enough. Consistent with the whole session: every
goal-induction path yields a sparse/terminal signal, never the dense steering the hard
levels need.

verdict: complete: hindsight_pair_goal_no_L2_advantage_honest_null_gap_sharpened

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>
